<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">B. P. Koirala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bishweshwar Prasad Koirala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">B. P. Koirala (1914–1982) was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepali-congress">Nepali Congress</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>'s first democratically elected prime minister, taking office in 1959. In December 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendra-of-nepal">King Mahendra</a> dismissed his government and imprisoned him, introducing the partyless panchayat system. After years in prison and exile he returned in 1976 to campaign for democracy, and he was also an important writer of Nepali fiction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/b-p-koirala.jpg" alt="B. P. Koirala" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BP_Koirala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bishweshwar Prasad Koirala was born on 8 September 1914, the second son of Krishna Prasad Koirala, a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, and was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Banaras</a>. In 1930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> charged him and his brother Matrika Prasad Koirala with contacts with terrorists, and they were held for three months. He took a bachelor's degree in Economics and Politics at Banaras Hindu University in 1934, the year he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, and a law degree at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1937. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the British interned him at Dhanbad for two years, from 1942 to 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1947 he founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the socialist Nepali National Congress, which in 1950 became the Nepali Congress Party. In March 1947 he crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> to help his brother Girija Prasad Koirala with the Biratnagar jute mill strike. They were arrested and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> on a slow 21-day walk across the hills, and were released after a 27-day hunger strike, popular protests and a request from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>.</p><p>He led the Revolution of 1951, which overthrew the 104-year-old Rana regime, and served nine months as Home Minister in the Rana-Congress coalition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendra-of-nepal">King Mahendra</a>'s new constitution allowed free elections in 1959, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepali-congress">Nepali Congress</a> took more than two-thirds of the seats in the lower house. He took office in May 1959 and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>'s delegation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><p>His land reforms, especially the revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a> laws, offended the landed aristocracy that had long dominated the army. On 15 December 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendra-of-nepal">King Mahendra</a> suspended the constitution, dissolved parliament, dismissed the cabinet, imposed direct rule and imprisoned Koirala and his closest colleagues. Koirala, though suffering from throat cancer, was held without trial until 1968, then lived in self-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Banaras</a> until December 1976. He was arrested on his return and charged with attempting armed revolution, and cleared of treason and sedition in March 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Koirala was a social democrat in politics and, by one account, an existentialist in literature. He held that political freedoms alone were not enough in a poor country without socio-economic change, and that only socialism could guarantee freedom and equal opportunity. His first Nepali short story, Chandrabadan, appeared in 1935, and by 1938 he was an important short story writer, noted for psychological insight. His collection Doshi Chashma came out in 1949, and he wrote novels in prison between 1960 and 1968, among them Teen Ghumti, Sumnima and Modiain, which views the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> war through a war widow.</p><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> on 21 July 1982. An estimated half a million people attended his funeral. His writings were banned for many years, and the B. P. Koirala Memorial Trust was established in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “B. P. Koirala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a brief experiment with parliamentary government following the elections of 1959, King Mahendra dismissed the cabinet of <b>B.P. Koirala</b> in December 1960 and introduced the partyless panchayat system under the constitution of 1962, which concentrated power in the palace until 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1958" aria-label="Varanasi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6749 1232 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of B. P. Koirala"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7199 1502h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7199 1502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
